//! Dense numeric `Vector` and `Matrix` values over semantic Core Numbers.
//!
//! The module exports the frozen standard prototypes `Vector` and `Matrix`.
//! They are available only through an explicit import; there is no Prelude
//! binding:
//!
//! ```protos
//! LA: import("std:math/LinearAlgebra")
//! Vector: LA.Vector
//! Matrix: LA.Matrix
//! v: Vector(1, 2, 3)
//! m: Matrix(2, 3, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6)
//! ```
//!
//! Bind the members before calling them: `LA.Vector(1)` is a message send
//! whose selected slot is a prototype rather than a Closure, so it signals an
//! Error under ordinary message-send rules.
//!
//! Every Vector and Matrix is an ordinary object with ordinary identity that
//! delegates to its standard prototype. Its numbers are private storage, not
//! slots. Equality and hashing are ordinary identity unless a program
//! redefines them.
//!
//! ## Elements
//!
//! An element must be a genuine Number: an Integer, a Fraction, a Float, or a
//! Complex, possibly of different families in one value. `null`, Booleans,
//! Strings, and objects that merely delegate to a Number prototype are
//! rejected with an Error. Nothing is converted and no guest code runs.
//!
//! ## Vector
//!
//! - `Vector(x, ...)` answers a Vector of exactly the supplied elements, in
//!   order: `Vector()` is empty and `Vector(3)` holds the single element `3`.
//!   Explicit spread works: `Vector(...[1, 2, 3])`.
//! - `size()` answers the length.
//! - `at(i)` and `v[i]` answer the element at `0 <= i < size()`.
//! - `atPut(i, value)` replaces one element and answers `value`.
//! - `add(other)` answers the element-wise sum with a Vector of equal size.
//! - `scale(number)` answers every element multiplied by `number`.
//! - `dot(other)` answers the Hermitian inner product with a Vector of equal
//!   size: `sum(conjugate(self[i]) * other[i])`. Only the receiver's elements
//!   are conjugated: `Vector(Complex(0, 1)).dot(Vector(Complex(0, 1)))`
//!   is `1`.
//! - `copy()` answers an independent Vector with the same elements.
//!
//! ## Matrix
//!
//! - `Matrix(rows, columns, ...values)` answers a `rows × columns` Matrix of
//!   exactly `rows * columns` values in row-major order; any other count fails.
//!   `Matrix(0, n)` and `Matrix(n, 0)` are distinct empty shapes.
//! - `rows()` and `columns()` answer the dimensions as supplied.
//! - `at(row, column)` answers the element at `0 <= row < rows()` and
//!   `0 <= column < columns()`. There is no `m[row]` or `m[row, column]` form.
//! - `atPut(row, column, value)` replaces one element and answers `value`.
//! - `add(other)` answers the element-wise sum with a Matrix of the same shape.
//! - `scale(number)` answers every element multiplied by `number`.
//! - `matvec(vector)` answers the Vector of size `rows()` of
//!   `sum(self.at(row, k) * vector[k])`; `vector.size()` must be `columns()`.
//! - `matmul(other)` answers the `rows() × other.columns()` Matrix of
//!   `sum(self.at(row, k) * other.at(k, column))`; `other.rows()` must be
//!   `columns()`.
//! - `transpose()` answers the independent `columns() × rows()` transpose.
//! - `copy()` answers an independent Matrix with the same shape and elements.
//!
//! `matvec` and `matmul` conjugate nothing.
//!
//! ```protos
//! m: Matrix(2, 2, 1, 2, 3, 4)
//! m.matvec(Vector(1, 1))            // Vector(3, 7)
//! m.matmul(Matrix(2, 1, 1, 0))      // Matrix(2, 1, 1, 3)
//! m.transpose().at(0, 1)               // 3
//! ```
//!
//! ## Rules
//!
//! Indexes and dimensions are exact non-negative Integers of any size; a
//! negative, non-Integer, or out-of-range index signals an Error. Shapes must
//! match exactly: there is no broadcasting, reshaping, padding, or dtype.
//!
//! `atPut` checks that the receiver is not frozen, then the bounds, then the
//! value, and a failed write changes nothing. A closed Vector or Matrix still
//! accepts `atPut` on existing elements; a frozen one rejects it.
//!
//! Every Vector or Matrix result is a fresh open object that shares no
//! storage with its operands, and no operation mutates an operand.
//!
//! Each scalar operation is the standard Number arithmetic, including the
//! coupled Complex multiplication. Every contraction (`dot`, each `matvec`
//! element, each `matmul` cell) sums its products in ascending index order,
//! starting from the first product, never from `0` or `0.0`; an empty
//! contraction answers the exact Integer `0`. Float results therefore follow
//! that exact order:
//!
//! ```protos
//! Vector(1.0e16, 1.0, -1.0e16).dot(Vector(1, 1, 1))   // 0.0
//! Vector(-1.0).dot(Vector(0.0))                       // -0.0
//! Vector().dot(Vector())                              // 0
//! ```

// D214 C1/S1: `Vector` and `Matrix` are the exact frozen runtime-owned standard
// prototypes, installed as initial members of this module before this source
// executes. They are not redeclared here so that every module instance
// publishes the same prototypes.
